# lens_aberration.cyr -- third- and fifth-order transverse aberration of a surface sequence:
# the Seidel sums and Buchdahl's fifth-order sums, surface by surface
# Usage: include "lib/lens_aberration.cyr"
#   (requires error.cyr, ray_core.cyr, ray_fresnel.cyr, ray_trace.cyr, ray_system.cyr)
#
# New in 2.14.0, and NOT a port: the Rust 1.x crate had only the thin-lens Seidel
# coefficients that lens.cyr still carries. This takes a Prescription and its paraxial
# marginal and chief rays and returns, for every surface and for the system, the third-order
# (Seidel) and fifth-order (Buchdahl) coefficients of the transverse ray aberration, each
# fifth-order term split into the part the surface makes on its own (intrinsic) and the part
# it makes from the aberration it is handed (induced).
#
# THE EXPANSION, in Rimmer's letters as J. Sasian gives them (OPTI 517, "Higher-order
# aberrations", the Buchdahl-Rimmer slide):
#   eps_y = B cos(phi) rho^3 + F (2 + cos 2phi) rho^2 H + (3C + PI) cos(phi) rho H^2 + E H^3
#         + B5 cos(phi) rho^5 + (F1 + F2 cos 2phi) rho^4 H
#         + (M1 + M2 + M3 cos^2 phi) cos(phi) rho^3 H^2 + (N1 + N2 cos^2 phi) rho^2 H^3
#         + (5 C5 + PI5) cos(phi) rho H^4 + E5 H^5
#   eps_x = B sin(phi) rho^3 + F sin(2phi) rho^2 H + (C + PI) sin(phi) rho H^2
#         + B5 sin(phi) rho^5 + F2 sin(2phi) rho^4 H + (M2 + M3 cos^2 phi) sin(phi) rho^3 H^2
#         + N3 sin(2phi) rho^2 H^3 + (C5 + PI5) sin(phi) rho H^4
# B, F, C, PI, E are spherical aberration, coma, astigmatism, Petzval and distortion. Of the
# fifth order, B5 is spherical, F1 and F2 coma, M1 to M3 oblique spherical, N1 to N3
# elliptical coma, C5 and PI5 astigmatism and Petzval, E5 distortion. There is no settled
# naming past these letters, so none is offered.
#
# COORDINATES — read these before comparing a value against another program:
# - A ray is rho_vec times the paraxial marginal ray plus H times the chief ray, as straight
#   lines in object space in (height, direction tangent). So at the paraxial entrance pupil
#   its height is rho_vec times the marginal ray's; at a finite object, H times the chief
#   ray's; for an object at infinity its direction tangent is H times the chief ray's slope.
# - rho_vec = (rho sin(phi), rho cos(phi)): phi runs from +y, the meridional direction, and
#   H runs along y, in the y of the rays passed in.
# - eps is where the ray meets the paraxial image plane, minus the paraxial image point
#   H ybar', in lens units. Every coefficient is the aberration at rho = H = 1, so it scales
#   with the rays passed in.
# - ⚠ THE SIGN OF A TERM ODD IN H (F, E; F1, F2, N1 to N3, E5) is the chief ray's: it turns
#   over with the chief ray, as the image point H ybar' does. So read it against the sign of
#   ybar' (AberrSums_image_height): with ybar' > 0, E < 0 is barrel distortion; with
#   ybar' < 0, barrel distortion is E > 0. The terms even in H do not depend on it.
# - u is the slope dy/dz, nu = n u, and refraction is nu' = nu - y phi, as ray_system.
#
# THE SEIDEL SUMS are Welford's per-surface forms, with A = n (u + y c), Abar likewise,
# H_L = y nubar - nu ybar (the Lagrange invariant) and Delta the change across the surface:
#   S_I = -A^2 y Delta(u/n)    S_II = -A Abar y Delta(u/n)    S_III = -Abar^2 y Delta(u/n)
#   S_IV = -H_L^2 c Delta(1/n)    S_V = (Abar/A)(S_III + S_IV)
# In this sign convention B = S_I/(2 n'u'), and F, C, PI, E likewise from S_II to S_V, with
# n'u' the marginal ray's reduced slope in image space. Each surface's S equals its own third
# order here exactly, not only in the total.
#
# HOW (Buchdahl's method). In the variables where moving between surfaces is exactly linear —
# the height on each surface's tangent plane and n times the direction tangent — a real ray's
# coordinates (A, B) against the paraxial marginal and chief rays, which Lagrange brackets
# give, are constant between surfaces and change only at a refraction. At the paraxial image
# plane the ray's height is B ybar', so eps is ybar' times the sum of those changes: a sum over
# surfaces, exactly. Each change is a function of the (A, B) the surface receives.
# - Its cubic part is the surface's Seidel term.
# - Its quintic part, at the object-space (rho, H), is the INTRINSIC fifth order.
# - The INDUCED fifth order is the cubic part's response to the third-order error already in
#   the incoming A and B: the pupil and image aberrations of every surface before it.
# The refraction is expanded in truncated series in the invariants A.A, A.B and B.B of the
# incoming ray, by series arithmetic on the exact ray-sphere intersection and Snell's law. No
# fifth-order formula is typed in.
#
# ⚠ THE SPLIT BETWEEN SURFACES depends on those variables, and another program's surface
# contributions may differ from these by terms that cancel in the total. The system totals do
# not depend on it: they are the expansion of a measured quantity in the coordinates above.
#
# MEASURED against a 100-digit real-ray trace (3D vector refraction, not this expansion) over
# seven systems:
# - a seven-surface triplet with a dummy stop;
# - a singlet behind a remote stop with a finite object;
# - a cemented doublet;
# - an object in water;
# - the same with a stop between two lenses;
# - an aplanatic surface;
# - a plate in a converging beam.
# Every surface's third order, intrinsic and induced fifth order, and every system total, is
# within 1.3e-15 (third order) and 1.2e-14 (fifth) of the largest surface term of its order. A
# small coefficient is a cancellation of large surface terms, and its relative error is larger
# by that ratio. An independent review's own 90-digit trace agreed over 13 systems.

var ABERR3_B  = 0;      # spherical aberration (S_I)
var ABERR3_F  = 1;      # coma (S_II)
var ABERR3_C  = 2;      # astigmatism (S_III)
var ABERR3_PI = 3;      # Petzval (S_IV)
var ABERR3_E  = 4;      # distortion (S_V)

var ABERR5_B5  = 0;
var ABERR5_F1  = 1;
var ABERR5_F2  = 2;
var ABERR5_M1  = 3;
var ABERR5_M2  = 4;
var ABERR5_M3  = 5;
var ABERR5_N1  = 6;
var ABERR5_N2  = 7;
var ABERR5_N3  = 8;
var ABERR5_C5  = 9;
var ABERR5_PI5 = 10;
var ABERR5_E5  = 11;

# aberr_fifth's part: intrinsic + induced, or one of them.
var ABERR_TOTAL     = 0;
var ABERR_INTRINSIC = 1;
var ABERR_INDUCED   = 2;

# The surface index that names the whole system.
var ABERR_SYSTEM = (0 - 1);

# A row is 29 f64: the third order (5), then the intrinsic (12) and induced (12) fifth order.
var _AB_ROW = 29;

# 2^-40. A paraxial slope or height that a cancellation leaves within this fraction of the terms
# it was formed from is a zero that rounding has not reached: the 2.14.0 review found a system
# afocal to 1 ulp answered with an image 2.9e17 away, and a stop 2 ulps short of a focus with a
# marginal ray 1.35e16 high.
var _AB_NOISE = 0x3D70000000000000;

# The result of aberr_sums. count surfaces; lagrange = y nubar - nu ybar; image_height = ybar'
# and image_distance = the paraxial image plane's distance past the last vertex; image_nu =
# n'u' of the marginal ray there; rows = count + 1 rows of _AB_ROW f64, the last the system.
#derive(accessors)
struct AberrSums { count; lagrange; image_height; image_distance; image_nu; rows; }

# ── Truncated series ────────
# A series is 10 f64 (80 bytes): the coefficients of 1, a, b, c, a^2, ab, ac, b^2, bc, c^2 in
# the invariants a = A.A, b = A.B, c = B.B. Times A or B, its linear part is of third order in
# the ray and its quadratic part of fifth; products drop the rest. Every function here may
# write its result over an operand.

# 1 if x is finite (not NaN, not +-inf).
fn _ab_finite(x) {
    if ((x & _PRK_ABS) >= _PRK_INF) { return 0; }
    return 1;
}

# x, with -0 made +0: a coefficient that is zero is reported as +0, whatever sign scaled it.
fn _ab_pz(x) {
    if ((x & _PRK_ABS) == 0) { return 0; }
    return x;
}

# x = the constant k.
fn _abt_const(x, k) {
    store64(x, k);
    for (var i = 1; i < 10; i = i + 1) { store64(x + i * 8, 0); }
    return 0;
}

# x = la a + lb b + lc c.
fn _abt_lin(x, la, lb, lc) {
    _abt_const(x, 0);
    store64(x + 8, la);
    store64(x + 16, lb);
    store64(x + 24, lc);
    return 0;
}

# z = kx x + ky y.
fn _abt_comb(z, x, kx, y, ky) {
    for (var i = 0; i < 10; i = i + 1) {
        store64(z + i * 8, f64_add(f64_mul(kx, load64(x + i * 8)), f64_mul(ky, load64(y + i * 8))));
    }
    return 0;
}

# z = x + y.
fn _abt_add(z, x, y) { return _abt_comb(z, x, F64_ONE, y, F64_ONE); }

# z = x - y.
fn _abt_sub(z, x, y) { return _abt_comb(z, x, F64_ONE, y, f64_neg(F64_ONE)); }

# x's linear part alone.
fn _abt_lin_of(z, x) { return _abt_lin(z, load64(x + 8), load64(x + 16), load64(x + 24)); }

# z = k x.
fn _abt_scale(z, x, k) {
    for (var i = 0; i < 10; i = i + 1) { store64(z + i * 8, f64_mul(k, load64(x + i * 8))); }
    return 0;
}

# z = x + k.
fn _abt_addc(z, x, k) {
    for (var i = 1; i < 10; i = i + 1) { store64(z + i * 8, load64(x + i * 8)); }
    store64(z, f64_add(load64(x), k));
    return 0;
}

# z = x y. Every operand is loaded before z is written, so z may be x or y. Inlined, with the
# order of operations fixed: three helper calls a coefficient cost 122 ns a product, this 46 ns,
# and the results are identical to the bit.
fn _abt_mul(z, x, y) {
    var x0 = load64(x);
    var x1 = load64(x + 8);
    var x2 = load64(x + 16);
    var x3 = load64(x + 24);
    var y0 = load64(y);
    var y1 = load64(y + 8);
    var y2 = load64(y + 16);
    var y3 = load64(y + 24);
    var t1 = f64_add(f64_mul(x0, y1), f64_mul(x1, y0));
    var t2 = f64_add(f64_mul(x0, y2), f64_mul(x2, y0));
    var t3 = f64_add(f64_mul(x0, y3), f64_mul(x3, y0));
    # aa, ab, ac, bb, bc, cc: x0 y_k + x_k y0 plus the products of the linear parts
    var t4 = f64_add(f64_add(f64_mul(x0, load64(y + 32)), f64_mul(load64(x + 32), y0)), f64_mul(x1, y1));
    var t5 = f64_add(f64_add(f64_mul(x0, load64(y + 40)), f64_mul(load64(x + 40), y0)),
      f64_add(f64_mul(x1, y2), f64_mul(x2, y1)));
    var t6 = f64_add(f64_add(f64_mul(x0, load64(y + 48)), f64_mul(load64(x + 48), y0)),
      f64_add(f64_mul(x1, y3), f64_mul(x3, y1)));
    var t7 = f64_add(f64_add(f64_mul(x0, load64(y + 56)), f64_mul(load64(x + 56), y0)), f64_mul(x2, y2));
    var t8 = f64_add(f64_add(f64_mul(x0, load64(y + 64)), f64_mul(load64(x + 64), y0)),
      f64_add(f64_mul(x2, y3), f64_mul(x3, y2)));
    var t9 = f64_add(f64_add(f64_mul(x0, load64(y + 72)), f64_mul(load64(x + 72), y0)), f64_mul(x3, y3));
    store64(z, f64_mul(x0, y0));
    store64(z + 8, t1);
    store64(z + 16, t2);
    store64(z + 24, t3);
    store64(z + 32, t4);
    store64(z + 40, t5);
    store64(z + 48, t6);
    store64(z + 56, t7);
    store64(z + 64, t8);
    store64(z + 72, t9);
    return 0;
}

# Quadratic coefficient k (4 to 9) of e^2, for e with no constant term and linear part e1, e2, e3.
fn _abt_sq2(k, e1, e2, e3) {
    if (k == 4) { return f64_mul(e1, e1); }
    if (k == 5) { return f64_mul(F64_TWO, f64_mul(e1, e2)); }
    if (k == 6) { return f64_mul(F64_TWO, f64_mul(e1, e3)); }
    if (k == 7) { return f64_mul(e2, e2); }
    if (k == 8) { return f64_mul(F64_TWO, f64_mul(e2, e3)); }
    return f64_mul(e3, e3);
}

# z = f(x) for a function with f(x0 (1 + e)) = f0 (1 + d1 e + d2 e^2 + ...). x0 must be non-zero.
fn _abt_func(z, x, f0, d1, d2) {
    var x0 = load64(x);
    var e1 = f64_div(load64(x + 8), x0);
    var e2 = f64_div(load64(x + 16), x0);
    var e3 = f64_div(load64(x + 24), x0);
    var t[80];
    store64(&t, F64_ONE);
    for (var k = 1; k < 10; k = k + 1) {
        var v = f64_mul(d1, f64_div(load64(x + k * 8), x0));
        if (k > 3) { v = f64_add(v, f64_mul(d2, _abt_sq2(k, e1, e2, e3))); }
        store64(&t + k * 8, v);
    }
    for (var i = 0; i < 10; i = i + 1) { store64(z + i * 8, f64_mul(f0, load64(&t + i * 8))); }
    return 0;
}

# z = 1/x.
fn _abt_inv(z, x) { return _abt_func(z, x, f64_div(F64_ONE, load64(x)), f64_neg(F64_ONE), F64_ONE); }

# z = sqrt(x), for x0 > 0. 0x3FC0000000000000 is 1/8.
fn _abt_sqrt(z, x) { return _abt_func(z, x, f64_sqrt(load64(x)), F64_HALF, f64_neg(0x3FC0000000000000)); }

# ── One surface ────────

# The refraction at one surface, expanded. The incoming paraxial marginal ray is (y, u) and the
# chief ray (yb, ub), as heights on the vertex plane and direction tangents; n -> n2 are the
# media, c the curvature, nu2 and nub2 the rays' reduced slopes after, il = 1/[m, c]. A ray
# arriving as A m + B c, in the variables of the section header, leaves with its tangent-plane
# height and n2 times its direction tangent changed from the paraxial refraction's by
#   Y alpha + T beta  and  Y gamma + T delta,  Y = y A + yb B,  T = u A + ub B,
# where, with t the axial depth of the hit, D = cos(I) sqrt(1 + T.T), G = n2 cos(I') - n cos(I)
# and W = n/sqrt(1 + T.T) + G (1 - c t),
#   beta = t G/W   alpha = c beta   gamma = (n2 - n) c - n2 c G/W
#   delta = n2 (n/sqrt(1 + T.T) - c G t)/W - n
# Writes four series to out (320 bytes): P and Q, the change in B as A P + B Q, then PA and QA,
# the change in A as A PA + B QA.
fn _ab_surface(y, u, yb, ub, n, n2, c, nu2, nub2, il, out) {
    var xi[80];
    var eta[80];
    var zeta[80];
    var opz[80];
    var s[80];
    var s2[80];
    var omc[80];
    var arg[80];
    var dd[80];
    var t[80];
    var g[80];
    var w[80];
    var tmp[80];
    var al[80];
    var be[80];
    var ga[80];
    var de[80];
    _abt_lin(&xi, f64_mul(y, y), f64_mul(F64_TWO, f64_mul(y, yb)), f64_mul(yb, yb));          # Y.Y
    _abt_lin(&eta, f64_mul(y, u), f64_add(f64_mul(y, ub), f64_mul(yb, u)), f64_mul(yb, ub)); # Y.T
    _abt_lin(&zeta, f64_mul(u, u), f64_mul(F64_TWO, f64_mul(u, ub)), f64_mul(ub, ub));       # T.T
    _abt_addc(&opz, &zeta, F64_ONE);
    _abt_inv(&s2, &opz);
    _abt_sqrt(&s, &s2);                                  # 1/sqrt(1 + T.T)
    _abt_scale(&omc, &eta, f64_neg(c));
    _abt_addc(&omc, &omc, F64_ONE);                      # 1 - c Y.T
    _abt_mul(&arg, &omc, &omc);
    _abt_mul(&tmp, &xi, &opz);
    _abt_scale(&tmp, &tmp, f64_mul(c, c));
    _abt_sub(&arg, &arg, &tmp);                          # (1 - c Y.T)^2 - c^2 Y.Y (1 + T.T)
    _abt_sqrt(&dd, &arg);                                # D
    _abt_add(&tmp, &omc, &dd);
    _abt_inv(&tmp, &tmp);
    _abt_mul(&t, &xi, &tmp);
    _abt_scale(&t, &t, c);                               # t = c Y.Y / ((1 - c Y.T) + D)
    _abt_mul(&g, &arg, &s2);
    _abt_scale(&g, &g, f64_mul(n, n));
    _abt_addc(&g, &g, f64_sub(f64_mul(n2, n2), f64_mul(n, n)));
    _abt_sqrt(&g, &g);                                   # n2 cos(I')
    _abt_mul(&tmp, &dd, &s);
    _abt_comb(&g, &g, F64_ONE, &tmp, f64_neg(n));        # G
    _abt_scale(&tmp, &t, f64_neg(c));
    _abt_addc(&tmp, &tmp, F64_ONE);
    _abt_mul(&w, &g, &tmp);
    _abt_comb(&w, &w, F64_ONE, &s, n);
    _abt_inv(&w, &w);                                    # 1/W
    _abt_mul(&be, &t, &g);
    _abt_mul(&be, &be, &w);                              # beta
    _abt_scale(&al, &be, c);                             # alpha
    _abt_mul(&ga, &g, &w);
    _abt_scale(&ga, &ga, f64_neg(f64_mul(n2, c)));
    _abt_addc(&ga, &ga, f64_mul(f64_sub(n2, n), c));     # gamma
    _abt_mul(&de, &g, &t);
    _abt_comb(&de, &s, n, &de, f64_neg(c));
    _abt_mul(&de, &de, &w);
    _abt_scale(&de, &de, n2);
    _abt_addc(&de, &de, f64_neg(n));                     # delta
    # The brackets: the change in B is [m', dr]/[m, c] = (y dmom - nu2 dpos) il, the change in A
    # is [dr, c']/[m, c] = (nub2 dpos - yb dmom) il.
    _abt_comb(&xi, &ga, y, &al, f64_neg(nu2));           # B's coefficient of Y
    _abt_comb(&eta, &de, y, &be, f64_neg(nu2));          # B's coefficient of T
    _abt_comb(&zeta, &al, nub2, &ga, f64_neg(yb));       # A's coefficient of Y
    _abt_comb(&opz, &be, nub2, &de, f64_neg(yb));        # A's coefficient of T
    _abt_comb(out, &xi, f64_mul(il, y), &eta, f64_mul(il, u));
    _abt_comb(out + 80, &xi, f64_mul(il, yb), &eta, f64_mul(il, ub));
    _abt_comb(out + 160, &zeta, f64_mul(il, y), &opz, f64_mul(il, u));
    _abt_comb(out + 240, &zeta, f64_mul(il, yb), &opz, f64_mul(il, ub));
    return 0;
}

# out = sa P1 + sb Q1 + (X1's change), quadratic part: the fifth order a cubic part X1 (P1 or Q1)
# picks up when its invariants move by da, db, dc. P1, Q1, sa, sb are linear series.
fn _ab_induced1(x1, p1, q1, sa, sb, da, db, dc, out) {
    var tmp[80];
    _abt_mul(out, sa, p1);
    _abt_mul(&tmp, sb, q1);
    _abt_add(out, out, &tmp);
    _abt_comb(&tmp, da, load64(x1 + 8), db, load64(x1 + 16));
    _abt_add(out, out, &tmp);
    _abt_comb(out, out, F64_ONE, dc, load64(x1 + 24));
    return 0;
}

# The fifth order the cubic parts P1, Q1 of a surface pick up from the third-order error already
# in the coordinates it receives, A = rho (1 + SPA) + H SQA and B = rho SPB + H (1 + SQB), where
# SPA, SQA, SPB, SQB are the cubic sums of the surfaces before it (linear series). The changes
# in the invariants are
#   da = 2 (a SPA + b SQA)   db = a SPB + b SQB + b SPA + c SQA   dc = 2 (b SPB + c SQB)
# and the result is iP = SPA P1 + SPB Q1 + dP1, iQ = SQA P1 + SQB Q1 + dQ1.
fn _ab_induced(p1, q1, spa, sqa, spb, sqb, ip, iq) {
    var va[80];
    var vb[80];
    var vc[80];
    var da[80];
    var db[80];
    var dc[80];
    var tmp[80];
    _abt_lin(&va, F64_ONE, 0, 0);
    _abt_lin(&vb, 0, F64_ONE, 0);
    _abt_lin(&vc, 0, 0, F64_ONE);
    _abt_mul(&da, &va, spa);
    _abt_mul(&tmp, &vb, sqa);
    _abt_comb(&da, &da, F64_TWO, &tmp, F64_TWO);
    _abt_mul(&db, &va, spb);
    _abt_mul(&tmp, &vb, sqb);
    _abt_add(&db, &db, &tmp);
    _abt_mul(&tmp, &vb, spa);
    _abt_add(&db, &db, &tmp);
    _abt_mul(&tmp, &vc, sqa);
    _abt_add(&db, &db, &tmp);
    _abt_mul(&dc, &vb, spb);
    _abt_mul(&tmp, &vc, sqb);
    _abt_comb(&dc, &dc, F64_TWO, &tmp, F64_TWO);
    _ab_induced1(p1, p1, q1, spa, spb, &da, &db, &dc, ip);
    _ab_induced1(q1, p1, q1, sqa, sqb, &da, &db, &dc, iq);
    return 0;
}

# Adds Rimmer's twelve fifth-order letters, from the quadratic parts of P and Q, to row + 8 off.
fn _ab_add5(row, off, p, q) {
    var v[96];
    var pk = load64(p + 40);                            # P's ab coefficient
    var qk = load64(q + 64);                            # Q's bc coefficient
    store64(&v, load64(p + 32));                                            # B5 = p_aa
    store64(&v + 8, f64_add(load64(q + 32), f64_mul(F64_HALF, pk)));        # F1 = q_aa + p_ab/2
    store64(&v + 16, f64_mul(F64_HALF, pk));                                # F2 = p_ab/2
    store64(&v + 24, load64(q + 40));                                       # M1 = q_ab
    store64(&v + 32, load64(p + 48));                                       # M2 = p_ac
    store64(&v + 40, load64(p + 56));                                       # M3 = p_bb
    store64(&v + 48, load64(q + 48));                                       # N1 = q_ac
    store64(&v + 56, f64_add(load64(p + 64), load64(q + 56)));              # N2 = p_bc + q_bb
    store64(&v + 64, f64_mul(F64_HALF, load64(p + 64)));                    # N3 = p_bc/2
    store64(&v + 72, f64_mul(0x3FD0000000000000, qk));                      # C5 = q_bc/4
    store64(&v + 80, f64_sub(load64(p + 72), f64_mul(0x3FD0000000000000, qk))); # PI5 = p_cc - q_bc/4
    store64(&v + 88, load64(q + 72));                                       # E5 = q_cc
    for (var k = 0; k < 12; k = k + 1) {
        var a = row + (off + k) * 8;
        store64(a, f64_add(load64(a), load64(&v + k * 8)));
    }
    return 0;
}

# Adds the five third-order letters, from the linear parts of P and Q, to row.
fn _ab_add3(row, p, q) {
    var v[40];
    var qb = f64_mul(F64_HALF, load64(q + 16));
    store64(&v, load64(p + 8));                         # B = p_a
    store64(&v + 8, f64_mul(F64_HALF, load64(p + 16))); # F = p_b/2 (= q_a)
    store64(&v + 16, qb);                               # C = q_b/2
    store64(&v + 24, f64_sub(load64(p + 24), qb));      # PI = p_c - q_b/2
    store64(&v + 32, load64(q + 24));                   # E = q_c
    for (var k = 0; k < 5; k = k + 1) { store64(row + k * 8, f64_add(load64(row + k * 8), load64(&v + k * 8))); }
    return 0;
}

# ── API ────────

# Writes code to err_out and returns 0.
fn _ab_fail(err_out, code) {
    store64(err_out, code);
    return 0;
}

# 1 if x is finite and strictly positive. f64_gt is false for NaN.
fn _ab_pos(x) {
    if (f64_gt(x, 0) == 0) { return 0; }
    if (x == _PRK_INF) { return 0; }
    return 1;
}

# 1 if a prescription surface can be expanded: a radius that is not 0 or NaN (+-inf is flat),
# n_after finite and positive, and, unless last, a finite thickness.
fn _ab_surface_ok(ps, last) {
    if (ps == 0) { return 0; }
    var r = PrescriptionSurface_radius(ps) & _PRK_ABS;
    if ((r == 0) | (r > _PRK_INF)) { return 0; }
    if (_ab_pos(PrescriptionSurface_n_after(ps)) == 0) { return 0; }
    if (last == 1) { return 1; }
    return _ab_finite(PrescriptionSurface_thickness(ps));
}

# 1 if the prescription is non-null, non-empty, n_initial is finite and positive and every
# surface passes _ab_surface_ok.
fn _ab_prescription_ok(p) {
    if (p == 0) { return 0; }
    var count = prescription_len(p);
    if (count == 0) { return 0; }
    if (_ab_pos(Prescription_n_initial(p)) == 0) { return 0; }
    var surfs = Prescription_surfaces(p);
    for (var j = 0; j < count; j = j + 1) {
        var last = 0;
        if (j == count - 1) { last = 1; }
        if (_ab_surface_ok(vec_get(surfs, j), last) == 0) { return 0; }
    }
    return 1;
}

# Third- and fifth-order transverse aberration of a prescription for the paraxial marginal and
# chief rays `marginal` and `chief` (ParaxialRay: height at the first vertex and reduced slope
# in object space), surface by surface. See the section header for the coordinates: the
# coefficients are lengths at the paraxial image plane, at rho = H = 1. The last surface's
# thickness is ignored: the image is where the marginal ray crosses the axis.
# Writes PK_ERR_INVALID_PARAMETER for a null handle, an empty prescription, n_initial or an
# n_after that is not finite and positive, a radius of 0 or NaN, a thickness or ray value that
# is not finite, rays with a zero Lagrange invariant (no field or no aperture), or a result
# that overflows; PK_ERR_DIVISION_BY_ZERO if the image is at infinity (the marginal ray leaves
# parallel to the axis, to within 2^-40 of the slopes it was formed from); PK_ERR_ALLOCATION if
# memory runs out. Returns 0 on any of them. A coefficient that is zero is +0.
#must_use
fn aberr_sums(prescription, marginal, chief, err_out) {
    store64(err_out, PK_ERR_NONE);
    _prk_trace("aberr_sums");
    if ((marginal == 0) | (chief == 0)) { return _ab_fail(err_out, PK_ERR_INVALID_PARAMETER); }
    if (_ab_prescription_ok(prescription) == 0) { return _ab_fail(err_out, PK_ERR_INVALID_PARAMETER); }
    var y = ParaxialRay_y(marginal);
    var nu = ParaxialRay_nu(marginal);
    var yb = ParaxialRay_y(chief);
    var nub = ParaxialRay_nu(chief);
    if ((_ab_finite(y) & _ab_finite(nu) & _ab_finite(yb) & _ab_finite(nub)) == 0) {
        return _ab_fail(err_out, PK_ERR_INVALID_PARAMETER);
    }
    var lag = f64_sub(f64_mul(y, nub), f64_mul(nu, yb));
    var il = f64_div(F64_ONE, lag);
    if ((_ab_finite(lag) & _ab_finite(il)) == 0) { return _ab_fail(err_out, PK_ERR_INVALID_PARAMETER); }
    var count = prescription_len(prescription);
    var nvals = (count + 1) * _AB_ROW;
    var rows = alloc(nvals * 8);
    if (rows == 0) { return _ab_fail(err_out, PK_ERR_ALLOCATION); }
    for (var i = 0; i < nvals; i = i + 1) { store64(rows + i * 8, 0); }
    var ser[320];
    var p1[80];
    var q1[80];
    var pa1[80];
    var qa1[80];
    var ip[80];
    var iq[80];
    var spa[80];
    var sqa[80];
    var spb[80];
    var sqb[80];
    _abt_const(&spa, 0);
    _abt_const(&sqa, 0);
    _abt_const(&spb, 0);
    _abt_const(&sqb, 0);
    var surfs = Prescription_surfaces(prescription);
    var n = Prescription_n_initial(prescription);
    var nuscale = f64_abs(nu);                          # the largest slope n'u' was formed from
    for (var j = 0; j < count; j = j + 1) {
        var ps = vec_get(surfs, j);
        var n2 = PrescriptionSurface_n_after(ps);
        var c = f64_div(F64_ONE, PrescriptionSurface_radius(ps));
        var phi = f64_mul(f64_sub(n2, n), c);
        var nu2 = f64_sub(nu, f64_mul(y, phi));
        # Where nu' cancels, y phi is as large as the nu it cancels against, so nu alone sets the
        # scale (a mutant that also tracked y phi changed nothing the suite or a caller sees).
        if (f64_gt(f64_abs(nu), nuscale) == 1) { nuscale = f64_abs(nu); }
        var nub2 = f64_sub(nub, f64_mul(yb, phi));
        # Between equal indices a surface refracts nothing, and its row stays exactly +0.
        if (n2 != n) {
            _ab_surface(y, f64_div(nu, n), yb, f64_div(nub, n), n, n2, c, nu2, nub2, il, &ser);
            _abt_lin_of(&p1, &ser);
            _abt_lin_of(&q1, &ser + 80);
            _abt_lin_of(&pa1, &ser + 160);
            _abt_lin_of(&qa1, &ser + 240);
            _ab_induced(&p1, &q1, &spa, &sqa, &spb, &sqb, &ip, &iq);
            var row = rows + j * _AB_ROW * 8;
            _ab_add3(row, &ser, &ser + 80);
            _ab_add5(row, 5, &ser, &ser + 80);
            _ab_add5(row, 17, &ip, &iq);
            _abt_add(&spa, &spa, &pa1);
            _abt_add(&sqa, &sqa, &qa1);
            _abt_add(&spb, &spb, &p1);
            _abt_add(&sqb, &sqb, &q1);
        }
        if (j < count - 1) {
            var rt = f64_div(PrescriptionSurface_thickness(ps), n2);
            y = f64_add(y, f64_mul(rt, nu2));
            yb = f64_add(yb, f64_mul(rt, nub2));
        }
        nu = nu2;
        nub = nub2;
        n = n2;
    }
    # An image at infinity, or within rounding of it, is refused rather than placed 1e17 away.
    if (f64_le(f64_abs(nu), f64_mul(_AB_NOISE, nuscale)) == 1) { return _ab_fail(err_out, PK_ERR_DIVISION_BY_ZERO); }
    var dist = f64_neg(f64_div(f64_mul(y, n), nu));
    var hb = f64_add(yb, f64_mul(dist, f64_div(nub, n)));
    var ok = _ab_finite(dist) & _ab_finite(hb);
    var sys = rows + count * _AB_ROW * 8;
    for (var s = 0; s < count; s = s + 1) {
        for (var k = 0; k < _AB_ROW; k = k + 1) {
            var a = rows + (s * _AB_ROW + k) * 8;
            var v = _ab_pz(f64_mul(load64(a), hb));
            store64(a, v);
            store64(sys + k * 8, _ab_pz(f64_add(load64(sys + k * 8), v)));
        }
    }
    for (var i2 = 0; i2 < nvals; i2 = i2 + 1) { ok = ok & _ab_finite(load64(rows + i2 * 8)); }
    if (ok == 0) { return _ab_fail(err_out, PK_ERR_INVALID_PARAMETER); }
    var ab = alloc(sizeof(AberrSums));
    if (ab == 0) { return _ab_fail(err_out, PK_ERR_ALLOCATION); }
    AberrSums_set_count(ab, count);
    AberrSums_set_lagrange(ab, lag);
    AberrSums_set_image_height(ab, hb);
    AberrSums_set_image_distance(ab, dist);
    AberrSums_set_image_nu(ab, nu);
    AberrSums_set_rows(ab, rows);
    return ab;
}

# The row of surface `surface` (0 to count - 1) or of ABERR_SYSTEM; 0 for a null handle or an
# index out of range.
fn _ab_row(ab, surface) {
    if (ab == 0) { return 0; }
    var count = AberrSums_count(ab);
    var idx = surface;
    if (surface == ABERR_SYSTEM) { idx = count; }
    if ((idx < 0) | (idx > count)) { return 0; }
    if ((idx == count) & (surface != ABERR_SYSTEM)) { return 0; }
    return AberrSums_rows(ab) + idx * _AB_ROW * 8;
}

# Third-order coefficient k (ABERR3_*) of surface `surface` or ABERR_SYSTEM, in lens units.
# NaN for a null handle or an index out of range.
#must_use
fn aberr_third(ab, surface, k) {
    var row = _ab_row(ab, surface);
    if ((row == 0) | (k < 0) | (k > 4)) { return _PRK_NAN; }
    return load64(row + k * 8);
}

# Welford's Seidel sum S_I to S_V (k = ABERR3_*) of surface `surface` or ABERR_SYSTEM: the
# third-order coefficient times 2 n'u'. NaN for a null handle or an index out of range.
#must_use
fn aberr_seidel(ab, surface, k) {
    var t = aberr_third(ab, surface, k);
    if (ab == 0) { return _PRK_NAN; }
    return _ab_pz(f64_mul(f64_mul(F64_TWO, AberrSums_image_nu(ab)), t));
}

# Fifth-order coefficient k (ABERR5_*) of surface `surface` or ABERR_SYSTEM, in lens units:
# part ABERR_INTRINSIC, ABERR_INDUCED or their sum, ABERR_TOTAL. NaN for a null handle or an
# index or part out of range.
#must_use
fn aberr_fifth(ab, surface, k, part) {
    var row = _ab_row(ab, surface);
    if ((row == 0) | (k < 0) | (k > 11)) { return _PRK_NAN; }
    var intr = load64(row + (5 + k) * 8);
    var ind = load64(row + (17 + k) * 8);
    if (part == ABERR_INTRINSIC) { return intr; }
    if (part == ABERR_INDUCED) { return ind; }
    if (part == ABERR_TOTAL) { return f64_add(intr, ind); }
    return _PRK_NAN;
}

# The system's transverse aberration (eps_x, eps_y) at the fractional pupil point (px, py),
# py meridional, and fractional field h: the third order alone (order 3) or the third and the
# fifth (order 5). Writes 2 f64 to out and returns PK_ERR_NONE; on a null handle or out, an
# order other than 3 or 5, an input that is not finite or a result that overflows, writes NaN
# (when out is not null) and returns PK_ERR_INVALID_PARAMETER.
fn aberr_transverse(ab, px, py, h, order, out) {
    if (out == 0) { return PK_ERR_INVALID_PARAMETER; }
    store64(out, _PRK_NAN);
    store64(out + 8, _PRK_NAN);
    var row = _ab_row(ab, ABERR_SYSTEM);
    if ((row == 0) | ((order != 3) & (order != 5))) { return PK_ERR_INVALID_PARAMETER; }
    if ((_ab_finite(px) & _ab_finite(py) & _ab_finite(h)) == 0) { return PK_ERR_INVALID_PARAMETER; }
    var a = f64_add(f64_mul(px, px), f64_mul(py, py));
    var b = f64_mul(py, h);
    var c = f64_mul(h, h);
    var f = load64(row + 8);
    var cc = load64(row + 16);
    var p = f64_add(f64_add(f64_mul(load64(row), a), f64_mul(f64_mul(F64_TWO, f), b)),
      f64_mul(f64_add(cc, load64(row + 24)), c));
    var q = f64_add(f64_add(f64_mul(f, a), f64_mul(f64_mul(F64_TWO, cc), b)), f64_mul(load64(row + 32), c));
    if (order == 5) {
        var t[96];
        for (var k = 0; k < 12; k = k + 1) {
            store64(&t + k * 8, f64_add(load64(row + (5 + k) * 8), load64(row + (17 + k) * 8)));
        }
        var aa = f64_mul(a, a);
        var ab2 = f64_mul(a, b);
        var ac = f64_mul(a, c);
        var bb = f64_mul(b, b);
        var bc = f64_mul(b, c);
        var c2 = f64_mul(c, c);
        var f2 = load64(&t + 16);
        var n3 = load64(&t + 64);
        var c5 = load64(&t + 72);
        # P: B5 a^2 + 2 F2 ab + M2 ac + M3 b^2 + 2 N3 bc + (C5 + PI5) c^2
        p = f64_add(p, f64_mul(load64(&t), aa));
        p = f64_add(p, f64_mul(f64_mul(F64_TWO, f2), ab2));
        p = f64_add(p, f64_mul(load64(&t + 32), ac));
        p = f64_add(p, f64_mul(load64(&t + 40), bb));
        p = f64_add(p, f64_mul(f64_mul(F64_TWO, n3), bc));
        p = f64_add(p, f64_mul(f64_add(c5, load64(&t + 80)), c2));
        # Q: (F1 - F2) a^2 + M1 ab + N1 ac + (N2 - 2 N3) b^2 + 4 C5 bc + E5 c^2
        q = f64_add(q, f64_mul(f64_sub(load64(&t + 8), f2), aa));
        q = f64_add(q, f64_mul(load64(&t + 24), ab2));
        q = f64_add(q, f64_mul(load64(&t + 48), ac));
        q = f64_add(q, f64_mul(f64_sub(load64(&t + 56), f64_mul(F64_TWO, n3)), bb));
        q = f64_add(q, f64_mul(f64_mul(f64_from(4), c5), bc));
        q = f64_add(q, f64_mul(load64(&t + 88), c2));
    }
    var ex = f64_mul(px, p);
    var ey = f64_add(f64_mul(py, p), f64_mul(h, q));
    if ((_ab_finite(ex) & _ab_finite(ey)) == 0) { return PK_ERR_INVALID_PARAMETER; }
    store64(out, _ab_pz(ex));
    store64(out + 8, _ab_pz(ey));
    return PK_ERR_NONE;
}

# Paraxial height, at the vertex of surface k, of the ray with height y0 and reduced slope nu0 at
# the first vertex, or 0 when that height is within _AB_NOISE of the largest term it was summed
# from: a ray that reaches the stop only through rounding does not reach it. The surfaces before
# k must have passed _ab_surface_ok.
fn _ab_height_at(prescription, k, y0, nu0) {
    var surfs = Prescription_surfaces(prescription);
    var n = Prescription_n_initial(prescription);
    var y = y0;
    var nu = nu0;
    var scale = f64_abs(y0);
    for (var j = 0; j < k; j = j + 1) {
        var ps = vec_get(surfs, j);
        var n2 = PrescriptionSurface_n_after(ps);
        var c = f64_div(F64_ONE, PrescriptionSurface_radius(ps));
        nu = f64_sub(nu, f64_mul(y, f64_mul(f64_sub(n2, n), c)));
        var dy = f64_mul(f64_div(PrescriptionSurface_thickness(ps), n2), nu);
        y = f64_add(y, dy);
        if (f64_gt(f64_abs(dy), scale) == 1) { scale = f64_abs(dy); }
        if (f64_gt(f64_abs(y), scale) == 1) { scale = f64_abs(y); }
        n = n2;
    }
    if (f64_le(f64_abs(y), f64_mul(_AB_NOISE, scale)) == 1) { return 0; }
    return y;
}

# 1 if object_distance names an object at infinity (+-inf).
fn _ab_at_infinity(object_distance) {
    if ((object_distance & _PRK_ABS) == _PRK_INF) { return 1; }
    return 0;
}

# 1 if the inputs aberr_marginal_ray and aberr_chief_ray share are usable.
fn _ab_ray_inputs_ok(prescription, object_distance, stop_index) {
    if (_ab_prescription_ok(prescription) == 0) { return 0; }
    if ((stop_index < 0) | (stop_index >= prescription_len(prescription))) { return 0; }
    if ((object_distance & _PRK_ABS) > _PRK_INF) { return 0; }
    return 1;
}

# The paraxial marginal ray, at the first vertex in object space, for an aperture stop of radius
# stop_radius on the vertex plane of surface stop_index: from the axial object point at
# object_distance in front of the first vertex (negative for a virtual object), or parallel to
# the axis when object_distance is +-inf. Writes PK_ERR_INVALID_PARAMETER for an invalid
# prescription (as aberr_sums), a stop index out of range, a radius that is not finite and
# positive, a NaN distance, or a stop on an image of the object (to within rounding), where no
# ray reaches its edge; PK_ERR_ALLOCATION if memory runs out. Returns 0 on any of them.
#must_use
fn aberr_marginal_ray(prescription, object_distance, stop_index, stop_radius, err_out) {
    store64(err_out, PK_ERR_NONE);
    if ((_ab_ray_inputs_ok(prescription, object_distance, stop_index) & _ab_pos(stop_radius)) == 0) {
        return _ab_fail(err_out, PK_ERR_INVALID_PARAMETER);
    }
    var n0 = Prescription_n_initial(prescription);
    var y = 0;
    var nu = 0;
    if (_ab_at_infinity(object_distance) == 1) {
        y = f64_div(stop_radius, _ab_height_at(prescription, stop_index, F64_ONE, 0));
    } else {
        # The ray from the axial point with slope 1, to the stop's height.
        var sc = f64_div(stop_radius, _ab_height_at(prescription, stop_index, object_distance, n0));
        y = f64_mul(object_distance, sc);
        nu = f64_mul(n0, sc);
    }
    if ((_ab_finite(y) & _ab_finite(nu)) == 0) { return _ab_fail(err_out, PK_ERR_INVALID_PARAMETER); }
    if (((y | nu) & _PRK_ABS) == 0) { return _ab_fail(err_out, PK_ERR_INVALID_PARAMETER); }
    var r = paraxial_ray_new(y, nu);
    if (r == 0) { return _ab_fail(err_out, PK_ERR_ALLOCATION); }
    return r;
}

# The paraxial chief ray, at the first vertex in object space, through the centre of a stop on
# the vertex plane of surface stop_index: from the object point at height `field` (object
# distance as aberr_marginal_ray), or, for an object at infinity, with slope `field`, the
# tangent of the field angle. ⚠ The two read a positive field from opposite sides, as lens
# design programs do: a positive height is an object above the axis, a positive slope a ray
# rising from below it. With a positive lens the first images below the axis, the second above;
# the coefficients odd in H follow (see the section header). A stop at the back focus of what
# precedes it is telecentric in object space: with a finite object the chief ray leaves it
# parallel to the axis, and is returned; with an object at infinity the stop is on the object's
# image, and is refused. Errors as aberr_marginal_ray, with a field that is 0 or not finite
# refused in place of the radius.
#must_use
fn aberr_chief_ray(prescription, object_distance, field, stop_index, err_out) {
    store64(err_out, PK_ERR_NONE);
    if ((_ab_ray_inputs_ok(prescription, object_distance, stop_index) & _ab_finite(field)) == 0) {
        return _ab_fail(err_out, PK_ERR_INVALID_PARAMETER);
    }
    if ((field & _PRK_ABS) == 0) { return _ab_fail(err_out, PK_ERR_INVALID_PARAMETER); }
    var n0 = Prescription_n_initial(prescription);
    var y1s = _ab_height_at(prescription, stop_index, F64_ONE, 0);
    var y2s = _ab_height_at(prescription, stop_index, 0, n0);
    var yb = 0;
    var ub = 0;
    if (_ab_at_infinity(object_distance) == 1) {
        ub = field;
        yb = f64_neg(f64_div(f64_mul(ub, y2s), y1s));
    } else {
        # d is the stop height of the ray from the axial point with slope 1. The height is
        # field y2s/d, not field + distance ub: that subtraction lost 3.9% at a distance of 1e15.
        var d = _ab_height_at(prescription, stop_index, object_distance, n0);
        ub = f64_neg(f64_div(f64_mul(field, y1s), d));
        yb = f64_div(f64_mul(field, y2s), d);
    }
    var nub = f64_mul(n0, ub);
    if ((_ab_finite(yb) & _ab_finite(nub)) == 0) { return _ab_fail(err_out, PK_ERR_INVALID_PARAMETER); }
    var r = paraxial_ray_new(yb, nub);
    if (r == 0) { return _ab_fail(err_out, PK_ERR_ALLOCATION); }
    return r;
}
